Shared living often strains friendship when logistical expectations clash with emotional bonds. Your situation highlights three distinct friction points: ambiguous memory of agreements, incompatible schedules, and communication avoidance. To address the ambiguity, shift from verbal agreements to a visible tracking system, such as a whiteboard or shared app, where completion is recorded immediately. This removes the need to recall who promised what. For the changing schedules, consider moving from fixed days to task ownership or a flexible roster that is reviewed weekly rather than monthly. Regarding the avoiding friend, direct group pressure may reinforce their withdrawal. Instead, initiate a brief, low-stakes individual conversation focused on their current capacity rather than assigning blame. Re-establishing a sense of safety often precedes productive logistical planning.